#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6$?\n\n1. $(a-2, b-6)$\n2. $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n3. $(a+2, b-6)$\n4. $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4$.\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4$. After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6$. After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(a+2,b-6)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n$b=a^4$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\ninto this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (a + 2 - 2)^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= a^{4} - 6 \\\\\n&= b - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when\n\n$$\nx = a + 2\n$$\n\n, we get $y = b - 6$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1$. What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1$.\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,0+1)=(3,1)$.\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x)$. After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-(1)) = (3,-1)$.\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3$. After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(3,-1+3)=(3,2)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad f(3)=1\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&=-1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = -f(x) + 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x)$. What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/141/Source/Images/f409140a46a8034453948c18db9672e9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$. So, we need to find the image of $(2,6)$, the local maximum of $y=f(x)$, under the transformation.\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny = f(2x)-1\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6)$.\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x)$. After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6)$.\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1$. After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n$(1,6-1) = (1,5)$.\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcf626c8-d2f0-51ca-9639-a109a0224e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 2, b + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056270} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbef1e4-ce60-520e-9c28-990f7d66238b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056271} {:answer/id #uuid "3f395b37-6aad-5a65-baba-60a043ec61be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056272} {:answer/id #uuid "72236c18-ec22-5d4d-bed9-212581bfdeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a, b + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056273} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7c666d-f03f-5a4c-968c-f04e83032c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a - 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbef1e4-ce60-520e-9c28-990f7d66238b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a + 1, b + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056271} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df0fc45-813c-5486-99d4-ce01fcdd3aa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056269}] :question/id #uuid "933f2dda-2343-544a-8ef3-2160dd14e9e2" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-121220" :question/problem "The point $(a, b)$ lies on the curve $y = x^{2}$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x - 1)^{2} + 2$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3701706-a2ab-527c-a91b-e50ea0d2a4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056276} {:answer/id #uuid "c72b485c-f914-54c6-9862-ca2e77d067be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056277} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5c6281-97f3-5f1f-8de8-37713e91a6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056278} {:answer/id #uuid "ee21d9f5-a100-54a6-b810-c55d4f0b2cfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056279} {:answer/id #uuid "38bcd493-ab4a-5d22-b3e3-9133412002a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38bcd493-ab4a-5d22-b3e3-9133412002a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056280} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6cf1b2-47cd-5307-a39b-ef3cfe5bdbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056275}] :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/is-example false :question/math-aca